poudriere overlay: local-fixes
==============================

Local patches to ports that are broken in the base tree. Registered as a
poudriere ports tree and passed to bulk with -O, so poudriere looks here
BEFORE /usr/ports (common.sh:1447 _lookup_portdir) and falls through to the
base tree for everything else.

An overlay entry is the WHOLE port directory, not a diff: _lookup_portdir
returns the overlay path AS the port dir and does not merge with /usr/ports,
so distinfo, pkg-descr, pkg-plist and files/ must all be present.

The category Makefiles here exist only so `poudriere bulk -a` would enumerate
the overlay (_list_ports_dir, common.sh:9479, skips an overlay with no
top-level Makefile). Builds from an explicit list do not need them.


x11/xlockmore  --  restores `tar:xz` to USES
-------------------------------------------
Base tree resolves EXTRACT_SUFX to .tar.gz while distinfo holds the .tar.xz
hash, so fetch refuses:

    => xlockmore-5.88.tar.gz is not in .../distinfo.

Cause: ports commit 6cd3e0d85531 (Gleb Popov, 2026-09-08, PR 298092),
"xorg-cat.mk users: Drop tar:xz, which is default now", removed tar:xz from
178 ports. The premise holds for USES=xorg-cat, where Mk/Uses/xorg-cat.mk:71
sets EXTRACT_SUFX?=.tar.xz. xlockmore uses USES=xorg, and Mk/Uses/xorg.mk sets
nothing about tarballs -- so it fell back to the global .tar.gz default.
tar:xz had been correct for this port since 5.80 (Oct 2024).

Of the 178 swept ports, this is the only one actually broken: the other
non-xorg-cat port, x11-drivers/xf86-video-qxl, still resolves .tar.xz.
Checked by comparing resolved DISTFILES against distinfo, not by the USES
heuristic, which gave one false positive.

WHY IT MATTERS HERE: xlockmore is installed, xautolock requires it, and
~/.xinitrc:39 runs `xautolock -time 20 -locker lock` -- it is the screen lock.

EXIT CONDITION: drop this port from the overlay once the base tree's
x11/xlockmore/Makefile has tar:xz in USES again. Worth reporting upstream;
PR 298092 was right for 177 of 178.


security/py-authlib  --  removes a stray line-continuation backslash
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Base tree's Makefile ends the FLASK_RUN_DEPENDS block with a trailing ` \`,
so make continues the assignment onto the next line and swallows the
JOSE_RUN_DEPENDS= text as a literal token. Measured on the base tree:

    JOSE_RUN_DEPENDS  = <empty -- never assigned>
    FLASK_RUN_DEPENDS = ...py-flask... ...py-flask-sqlalchemy...
                        JOSE_RUN_DEPENDS=  py312-pycryptodomex>=3.10<4:...

JOSE is a real option (OPTIONS_DEFINE= CLIENTS DJANGO FLASK JOSE), so with
JOSE selected the pycryptodomex dependency is silently absent while FLASK
gains a bogus entry.

Cause: ports commit a6c7ebf9994b (Po-Chuan Hsieh, 2025-03-19), "security/
py-authlib: Add CLIENTS, DJANGO, FLASK and JOSE options". Present since
March 2025 and survived the bump to 1.8.0. Reported upstream by cal; the bug
report is open at the time of writing.

EXIT CONDITION: drop this port once the base tree's line no longer ends in a
backslash -- or simply once `make -C /usr/ports/security/py-authlib -V
JOSE_RUN_DEPENDS` is non-empty.


archivers/zpaqfranz  --  patches upstream's Makefile for install(1)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
Stage fails, exit 64, reproduced in the 2026-10-08 21:35 bulk as well as the
Oct 1 one:

    install  -s -m 555 -l s zpaqfranz <stage>/usr/local/bin/dir
    install: -l and -s may not be specified together

Cause is in UPSTREAM's Makefile, inside the distfile, not the port:

     8  BSD_INSTALL_PROGRAM?=  install -m 0555
    12      ${BSD_INSTALL_PROGRAM} ${PROG} ${DESTDIR}${BINDIR}
    13      ${BSD_INSTALL_PROGRAM} -l s ${PROG} ${DESTDIR}${BINDIR}/dir

Line 8 is `?=`, so the framework's value wins -- here `install -s -m 555`.
Line 13 appends `-l s` to it, asking install(1) to strip and symlink in one
call, which FreeBSD refuses. GNU coreutils install does not, which is likely
why upstream never saw it.

files/patch-Makefile replaces line 13 with `ln -sf`, which needs nothing from
the framework and makes the same relative symlink the port's own
`post-install-DIRCOMMAND-off: ${RM} ${STAGEDIR}${LOCALBASE}/bin/dir` already
assumes exists.

TURNING THE OPTION OFF IS NOT A WORKAROUND. bgone has
OPTIONS_FILE_UNSET+=DIRCOMMAND, but the port lets upstream create the symlink
and deletes it afterwards -- and the failure is in `install`, which runs before
any post-install target. The option is unreachable.

Verified: the patch dry-runs and applies clean against a pristine extraction of
zpaqfranz-55.12.tar.gz; the old line reproduces the exact error when driven with
the framework's real INSTALL_PROGRAM, and `ln -sf` returns 0 and creates the
symlink. The port carries no files/ directory in the base tree, so this is the
first patch it has ever had.

EXIT CONDITION: drop this port once the base tree's
archivers/zpaqfranz/files/ contains a patch for the same line, or once upstream
ships a Makefile whose line 13 does not pass -l to a stripping install. Worth
reporting upstream to franco@francocorbelli.com / the port maintainer, who is
the same person.


graphics/opencv  --  tells cmake where the JDK is
--------------------------------------------------
With JAVA selected, the build SUCCEEDS for 1h43m and then package fails, in the
2026-10-14 18:03 bulk:

    pkg-static: Unable to access file <stage>/usr/local/share/java/opencv4/
                libopencv_java4130.so:No such file or directory
    pkg-static: Unable to access file <stage>/usr/local/share/java/opencv4/
                opencv-4130.jar:No such file or directory

cmake had already said so, 3000 lines earlier, without failing:

    1319  -- Could NOT find JNI (missing: JAVA_INCLUDE_PATH JAVA_INCLUDE_PATH2
                                          AWT JVM)
    1690  --     JNI:                         NO
    1691  --     Java wrappers:               NO

Cause is the port, not upstream and not a missing JDK. Everything FindJNI wants
is installed -- openjdk25 was pulled in as ant's dependency and carries
include/jni.h, include/freebsd/jni_md.h, lib/libawt.so and
lib/server/libjvm.so. But FindJNI derives all four from _JAVA_HOME, and
CMakeFindJavaCommon.cmake sets that from exactly three sources in order: the
JAVA_HOME cmake variable, then $ENV{JAVA_HOME}, then an APPLE-only
/usr/libexec/java_home probe. On FreeBSD the third never fires, and the port
sets neither of the first two -- JAVA_MAKE_ENV passes JAVACMD, which reaches the
BUILD phase and not configure. JAVA_HOME appears zero times in the whole build
log.

So the option is advertised, silently produces nothing, and costs an hour and
three quarters before the plist entries it enables fail on files that were
never built. opencv's cmake does not treat BUILD_JAVA=ON plus JNI=NO as an
error, which is what makes it quiet.

NOT A REGRESSION IN THE PORT -- A JDK MOVE UNDER IT. The same port with JAVA on
built fine on 14-3 at 4.11.0_3, where ant brought openjdk8:

    1334  -- Found JNI: /usr/local/openjdk8/include  found components: AWT JVM
    1718  --     Java wrappers:               YES (ANT)

openjdk8 keeps libjvm under jre/lib/<arch>/server and FindJNI's unhinted guesses
reach it; a modular JDK (17, 25) keeps it under lib/server and they do not.
The missing hint has always been missing; it only became fatal when the default
JDK moved.

THE FIX is one line, JAVA_CMAKE_ON= -DJAVA_HOME:PATH=${JAVA_HOME}. The ports
Java framework already computes JAVA_HOME from the JDK the port itself depends
on (USES=java), so the value is right by construction and follows a future
default-JDK move without editing. With it set, FindJNI resolves all four:
include (FindJNI.cmake:357), include/freebsd for jni_md.h (:505-510),
lib for libawt (:227-231), and ${dir}/server for libjvm (:341-347).

A SECOND, UNRELATED CHANGE IS REQUIRED BY THE OVERLAY ITSELF, and it is not part
of the Java bug: the base Makefile reaches a sibling port by relative path,
`.include <../../math/vtk9/Makefile.version>` (for VTK_VER, used by
VTK_LIB_DEPENDS and read even when VTK is off). Because an overlay entry
REPLACES the port dir rather than merging, that path resolves inside the overlay,
where math/ does not exist, and make dies before reading anything:

    make: .../graphics/opencv/Makefile:258: Could not find
          ../../math/vtk9/Makefile.version

Spelled "${PORTSDIR}/math/vtk9/Makefile.version" it resolves to the base tree
from either location. Deliberately NOT solved by adding math/vtk9/ to the
overlay: the overlay is searched first, so a partial vtk9 directory risks
capturing lookups for the real port, which builds fine today.

VERIFIED, and the limit is stated because it matters: with JAVA on, the overlay
adds -DJAVA_HOME:PATH=/usr/local/openjdk17 to CMAKE_ARGS and the base tree adds
nothing (1 against 0); with JAVA off the overlay adds nothing either, so the
knob is gated on the option. JAVA_CMAKE_ON is a real options helper
(bsd.options.mk:98 and the list at :198) and real ports pass full -D strings
through it (graphics/blender, graphics/alembic). The port evaluates after the
include change (PORTNAME=opencv, VTK_VER=9.6). Every variable bgone and
poudriere read is byte-identical to the base tree -- PKGNAME, OPTIONS_DEFINE,
OPTIONS_DEFAULT, BUILD_DEPENDS, LIB_DEPENDS, RUN_DEPENDS, USES, PLIST_SUB --
and only CMAKE_ARGS differs. WHAT IS NOT VERIFIED: that cmake then actually
builds and stages the .so and the .jar. That needs the 1h43m build; the
mechanism is proven, the outcome is not.

bgone cost: NONE. It reads none of CMAKE_ARGS, and every variable it does read
is identical above.

EXIT CONDITION: drop this port once the base tree hands cmake the JDK itself --

    env WITH=JAVA make -C /usr/ports/graphics/opencv -V CMAKE_ARGS | grep -c JAVA_HOME

non-zero means the base tree has caught up, whichever mechanism it chose
(JAVA_CMAKE_ON, CONFIGURE_ENV, or explicit JAVA_INCLUDE_PATH/JAVA_JVM_LIBRARY).
Worth reporting upstream to the port maintainer: the option is unbuildable as
shipped on any modular JDK, and it fails late.


Verifying the overlay after a ports update
------------------------------------------
Both fixes are detectable in one command each, against the BASE tree:

    make -C /usr/ports/x11/xlockmore -V DISTFILES          # want .tar.xz
    make -C /usr/ports/security/py-authlib -V JOSE_RUN_DEPENDS   # want non-empty
    env WITH=JAVA make -C /usr/ports/graphics/opencv -V CMAKE_ARGS \
        | grep -c JAVA_HOME                                # want non-zero

If either now reports the fixed value, the base tree has caught up and that
port should leave the overlay -- a stale override is the thing an overlay is
most likely to go wrong by.


bgone does NOT see this overlay
-------------------------------
bgone carries ONE ports tree root (`-p/--ports-dir`, default /usr/ports; or
`--poudriere-ports <TREE>`, which is also one tree) and has no overlay flag --
not in --help, not in bgone.toml, and no overlay string in the binary. So when
you configure options, bgone evaluates the BASE tree.

Measured cost, by diffing every variable bgone reads, base vs overlay:

  x11/xlockmore        every one identical. Its defect is EXTRACT_SUFX, which
                       affects fetch and nothing bgone looks at. No cost.
  security/py-authlib  JOSE_RUN_DEPENDS and FLASK_RUN_DEPENDS differ.

So the cost is one port and it is LATENT: RUN_DEPENDS reads the same today only
because neither option is selected. Select JOSE in bgone and bgone will believe
JOSE adds no dependency; select FLASK and it shows a bogus `JOSE_RUN_DEPENDS=`
entry. That is exactly the decision bgone exists to inform.

IT DOES NOT BREAK THE BUILD. poudriere resolves the closure itself
(deps_fetch_vars, common.sh:7062) from the OVERLAID Makefile, so
py312-pycryptodomex is pulled in correctly even if bgone's list omits it.
(It is currently in neither the local repo nor local-all.txt, so it would be a
new build either way.)

`--poudriere-ports local-fixes` is mechanically accepted -- the overlay is a
registered tree -- but it has no fallthrough, so dependency resolution for
every other port fails. Not a usable workaround.

THE REAL FIX is an overlay flag in bgone, same shape as poudriere's: search the
overlays first, fall back to ports_dir. The root already flows through one place
(src/cli.rs:383, `derived.ports_dir = Some(poudriere.ports_dir(tree)?)`), so it
is a Vec<PathBuf> plus a first-match-wins lookup rather than a scattered change.
Until then: when configuring security/py-authlib, JOSE's pycryptodomex
dependency is real even though bgone shows none.
